  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Frontline Community Protection Capacity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to work in way that upholds the humanitarian systems commitments and aspirations regarding local humanitarian leadership and more equitable partnerships with national humanitarian actors as embodied by the Grand Bargain and Charter for Change as well as institution -specific localisation strategies and policies. Oxfam has a strong group of national protection partners at both strategic and operational level, including Congolese Non Governmental Organisation (NGO), Centre de D&#xE9;veloppement Int&#xE9;gral de l&apos;Enfant Rural  (CEDIER) which is one of four pioneering national NGOs recently elected to the Strategic Advisory Board of the Global Protection Cluster (GPC). This project will work in strategic partnerships with up to four national NGOs, with direct funding, mentoring, and coaching to take leadership on key activities including supporting the co -leadership of the Community Led Project Task Team (CLP TT), greater national representation in the Arms Trade Treaty (ATT) and an ambition to handover of co -leadership to a national NGO or network, and building greater national and Global South Monitoring Evaluation Accountability and Learning (MEAL) capacity for protection. This project will help increase national organisation&apos;s own capacity and skills, including through exchanges with other similar actors around the world, build stronger relationships to strengthen and fully capitalise on that connection, and support them to manage risk, as part of equal partnership, exchange, and relationship -building. This project proposes to help put the power, resources and strengthen the capacity of those with most to gain &#x2013; and lose &#x2013; from the effectiveness of humanitarian action. As members of the community protection committee in Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) told Oxfam: &quot;If we don&apos;t do it, who will?&quot;</narrative>
  </description>
